Ah, I see what you mean.

With your numbers; yes, you wouldn't dip 30. Also not 25, because
of the very bad odds. You'd have to choose whether you want to
accept something like a 10% failure rate, or even 20%. - It's not
that different from the learning rate for books; will I take the
risk at any given XL/Int that the spellbook paralyzes me or even
vanishes if it fails? - So you'd dip 10 or 15 potions, and if the
potions mix you have another chance. If they explode you may try
again with the next one of the small stack of (3 in your example)
potions. But that is no additional complexity; we already have
that. The main factor for the stack partitioning is the straight
probability that I am willing to risk. If you have many potions
(3 as in your example, or even more) you may make smaller stacks
of the larger stacks of potions of size N), and if they explode
you may again decide to increase the risk for the remaining stack
(N + N-1), or retry as intended with N potions and keep the N-1
unused ones for the next potion that you happen to find. But that
is no additional complexity compared to some formula depending on
two parameters in the first place.

